Date: Tue, 25 May 93 18:13:38 PDT
From: tribble@memex.com (E. Dean Tribble)
Subject: Is AI evidence for the existence of the soul?

It's been a long time since I've heard anyone claim that there was a
direct mechanistic implementation of consciousness.  The claims I find
most credible are that it is an emergent phenomenon of the complex
system that is our brain.  It's hardly surprising that it took a while
for this to sink in:  in any field that digs into complex systems,
the majority typically thinks the emergent phenomenon of the system is
*the real thing* and the pieces that make it up are irrelevant (does
macroeconomics spring to mind?).

If indeed consciousness is such an emergent phenomenon (as genetic
algorithm, neural nets, Dennett, etc. all suppose), then it's no
surprise we haven't built it yet:  we're working on hardware that is
many orders of magnitude slower than the hardware that convincingly
demonstrates consciousness.  If we're only 9 orders of magnitude
slower than human brains (a laughable underestimation), then an
algorithm that could speak intelligently wouldn't say a seconds worth
of stuff till it had run continuously for *31 years*.  We could
completely understand the process and it might not do us *any* good.

As such realizations sink in, people have scaled back their ambitions
and now strive to make competent bugs.  In the process of course,
we've explored many interesting and profitable areas of computing, but
the fact that we haven't succeeded yet doesn't say anything about our
abiity to.

dean

