Robin posted:

>The question is, what processes can create such interlocking
>expectations about who will defend what rights how much?  And given a
>choice of several such processes, which should we prefer?
>"Naturalness", whatever that means, should only be given so much
>weight.  No one needs to own something now for an auction to be able
>to create rights.  What is needed is shared expectations that people
>will defend these newly created rights.

The principle that ownership can be initiated by first possession is one that
has almost universal acceptance, at least at the macro-level.  Who would deny
that if you dig up a diamond from the ground that you own it?  Few.  This is
a process that yields such expectations.

There aren't any alternatives.  Consider a micro-level example: you and two
others wash up on a deserted island after a shipwreck.  How are you going to
establish ownership of things on the island?  Assume that you have stable
currency: you announce to your companions that you'll proceed to auction off
the whole island.  How do you expect them to react?

The only way you'll be able to make it work is if you take possession of the
whole island immediately before the others.  Imagine the difficulty of this!
This is supposed to be easier than homesteading?

Walrasian auctions entail State-enforcement in practice, and are thus subject to
the same objections.

Perry posted:

>I suspect that something analogous to riparian rights to waterways
>would arise for the atmosphere. As for species, well, you don't need
>to buy the whole thing -- just some individual members -- and we have
>mechanisms to cope with that right now.

Riparian rights operate on the "reasonable use" standard.  Out here in the
West, spontaneous order came up with the "first in use, first in right"
standard.  The latter is preferable.  "Reasonableness" is too vague, arbitrary,
and admitting of moral imperialism.  Under the Western standard, it wouldn't
matter what anyone evaluated your use, but that you were first in use.

Endangered species have already been privatized.  At least, many particular
members representative of entire species have.  The Wall St. Journal had an
Op-Ed about this that claimed that a Texan game rancher has preserved all 27
bloodlines of one kind of endangered African antelope species.

Along similar lines, a PBS documentary on the Siberian forest, the Taiga
