Date: Wed, 26 May 93 10:30:56 CDT
From: eder@hsvaic.boeing.com (Dani Eder)
Subject: Is AI evidence for the existence of the soul?

Sub-Subject: Possibility of success after repeated trials.

I would like to make an analogy to other types of engineering
on the question of attempts at human-equivalent AI.  I will
use a 'beanstalk', or geosynchronous tower as my example.

Clearly, this is a very difficult engineering challenge, just as
human-level AI seems to be.  There have been a number of
failures in structural engineering (collapse of gothic 
cathedrals, the Tacoma Narrows Bridge, the failure of the
Tethered Satellite mission).  These failures have not
reduced the chance of ever building a beanstalk.  In fact,
in each case we have learned more about building things.

The principal obstacle in building a beanstalk is we don't
have a sufficiently strong material to build it with.  I claim
the principal obstacle to human-equivalent AI is we don't
have a sufficiently fast and capacious computer to run
the program.

In the mean time, we continue to learn both from the successes,
and the failures, and we gradually get closer to the goals
in both areas.  Meanwhile, we should withhold judgement on
the ultimate probability of success until the strength of
materials or the speed and capacity of computers stabilize
by hitting a physical limit.

Dani Eder

