Date: Tuesday, 25 May 1993 21:07:57 PST8
From: "James A. Donald" <jamesdon@infoserv.com>
Subject: BRAIN:  Emulation of

In <9305241944.AA19920@wookumz.gnu.ai.mit.edu>, rb@hprrb.rose.hp.com (Robert Brooks)  wrote:

> And neurons are certainly more "foreign" to a Von Neumann computer than
> gates!  Thus, it seems appropriate to apply a derating factor of a 
> million to a billion to any analysis of simulating a brain on a 
> conventional computer, unless and until there is enough understanding 
> of the brain to allow emulation on a much higher level than individual 
> neurons.

Emulation of simple brains fails because we do not have sufficient
understanding of neurons, not because of insufficient computing power.
Emulation of simple reflexes and simple Pavlovian conditioned reflexes has
been successful. Emulations have generated accurate and unobvious predictions
of how a creature would react to specific and unusual forms of conditioning.

Simple animals often have fixed brain wiring - every healthy member of the
species has identical circuits, making it relatively easy to map their
brains. Such animals have very limited capacity to learn, but often have
complex and fluid innate behavior.  Neuron emulations successfully emulate
what those animals do poorly, and computers do well, such as learning simple
associations, but fail to emulate what animals do well and computers do
poorly, such as competent conduct in a fluid, irregular, and variable
environment.

Existing emulations have had limited success, but this does not show that
existing models of neurons are fundamentally wrong, merely that they are
wrong.  Existing results for emulations are not so bad as to constitute
evidence that a totally new approach to our understanding of individual
neurons is needed.  On the other hand the results from AI, or rather the lack
of results, *do* suggest that a totally new approach is needed.

Existing algorithms for perception are not capable of being executed in a
reasonable time on any possible hardware.   A decade or so ago people were
arguing that the trick was a large number of clever, domain specific, special
purpose algorithms.  But perception does not seem to be that domain specific.
If the trick is a clever algorithm or clever algorithms, then those
algorithms have very wide applicability.  If we are using algorithms with
universal or of wide applicability, then we should be able to figure out what
those algorithms are by looking at illusions, visual paradoxes, etcetera, but such
studies have not turned up anything unusual.

The fact that computers are very good at the things that animals are bad at,
and animals are very good at the things that computers are bad at, suggests
to me that there is something very odd about the hardware rather than
something very clever about the algorithms.

But it seems to me that the structure of the brain indicates that such a
hardware difference would have to exist at the neuron level, rather than the
whole brain level, because neurons mostly communicate with perfectly
straightforward signals.

A "soul" would mean that there was something very peculiar about the brain
as a whole.  But we understand quite well how one small part of the brain
signals another small part of the brain, so we should rather conclude that
there is something very peculiar about individual neurons, rather than
something very peculiar about the way they work together.

Continued attempts at emulating simple brains may eventually put this
question in clearer focus.

