1) AI researchers don't for the most part do neural net work. The few
   that do neural net work don't use neural models that even pretend
   to be realistic.

2) Neurons are certainly more complicated than the common computer
   models -- no one even pretends otherwise.

3) Neurons are well understood, relatively speaking.

4) The fact that you are inclined to believe anything about neurons,
   let alone that hundreds of neurobiologists are prfoundly mistaken,
   based simply on your hearsay about AI demonstrates an ignorance of
   both neurobiology AI research and a failure of logic.

> Many neurons collect data from radically discrepant sources, and output
> sensible results, which would indicate that some hidden processing is going
> on at level below that which we can detect.

Huh? This is just plain wrong. Natural neural networks can indeed
take data from lots of sources and do interesting things with them,
but individual neurons are very simple beasts, and I've never heard
anyone observe one do anything terribly inexplicable under the current
models of how neurons work. As for "hidden processing", to my
knowledge the functioning of neurons is fairly well understood as
cells go. (Many general features of cell function are still poorly
understood, but we are getting better at it every day -- and few
radical suprises are showing up.)

Perry Metzger

